BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:IU Jacobs School of Music
DTSTART:20260710T230000Z
DTEND:20260711T010000Z
LOCATION:Recital Hall
SUMMARY:William Harvey\, violin\; Malcolm Liu\, piano
DESCRIPTION:Repertoire  Messiaen: Thème et variations (1932)Fazil Say: S
 onata\, Op. 7 (1997)Sauret: 'A Péra' from "Souvenir d'Orient\, Op. 63" (
 1901)Gity Razaz: Creation of the Birds (2023)Cowell: Selections from "Hom
 age to Iran" (1957)Reena Esmail: When the Violin (2018/20)Rimsky-Korsakov
 /Powell: 'Song of the Indian Guest (Song of India)' from "Sadko" (1898)Ru
 o Huang: Four Fragments (2006)Takemitsu: Distance de fée (1951)Kreisler:
  Tambourin chinois\, Op. 3 (1910)About the Artists  Concertmaster of Mexi
 co's premier orchestra\, the Orquesta Sinfónica Nacional\, and violin fa
 culty at the Universidad Panamericana\, violinist\, conductor\, and compo
 ser William Harvey has appeared as violin soloist at Carnegie Hall with t
 he New York Youth Symphony and has performed concerti with orchestras in 
 Argentina\, El Salvador\, the Philippines\, Mexico\, and the United State
 s. His recording of the violin concerto by Hector Infanzón garnered two 
 nominations to the Latin Grammys in 2021. For four years\, he served as t
 he violin and viola teacher at Afghanistan National Institute of Music (A
 NIM)\, founding the Afghan Youth Orchestra (AYO)\, which he conducted for
  President Hamid Karzai and led on a historic tour of the United States i
 n concerts at Carnegie Hall and the Kennedy Center. Harvey also conducted
  AYO on the 2012 and 2013 season finales of Afghan Star. He also publishe
 d anthologies of Afghan songs arranged for strings. He has previously ser
 ved as concertmaster of the Orquesta Sinfónica Sinaloa Las Artes (Mexico
 )\, Orquesta Sinfónica de la Universidad Nacional de San Juan (Argentina
 ) and the Spokane Symphony\, faculty at Indiana University's Summer Strin
 g Academy\, fellow at Carnegie Hall's Academy\, and guest artist at the M
 yanmar Music Festival\, Mehli Mehta Music Foundation\, and Iguazu en Conc
 ierto. He has performed at festivals throughout Mexico\, including the Fe
 stival del Centro Histórico. As a conductor\, he has led professional or
 chestras throughout Mexico and youth orchestras in Argentina\, Qatar\, th
 e Philippines\, Zimbabwe\, and the United States. Harvey's compositions h
 ave received over a hundred performances. Harvey earned his M.M. from The
  Juilliard School\, where he studied violin with Ronald Copes and composi
 tion with Samuel Adler\, and B.M. from the IU Jacobs School of Music\, wh
 ere he studied violin with Mimi Zweig and composition with Sven-David San
 dström. In 2005\, Harvey founded Cultures in Harmony. CiH workshops in P
 akistan\, Qatar\, Egypt\, the Philippines\, Tunisia\, Zimbabwe\, and Mexi
 co have benefited thousands of young musicians. Harvey plays on violins m
 ade by Joseph Curtin (2001) and Nadia Sánchez (2021).Guobi Malcolm Liu i
 s currently pursuing a Doctor of Music degree in Collaborative Piano and 
 is an associate instructor at the Indiana University Jacobs School of Mus
 ic. He studies with Kevin Murphy and Anne Epperson in the Balanced Track 
 program option\, with equal experience in vocal and instrumental projects
 . Since fall 2025\, Liu has worked as a pianist/coach for the Graduate Op
 era Workshop at the Jacobs School\, directed by Carol Vaness and Zachary 
 Coates. Additionally\, he was rehearsal pianist and orchestral keyboardis
 t for the Jacobs Opera Theater production of Mansfield Park this past sea
 son under guest conductor Gary Matthewman. In May 2026\, he served as reh
 earsal pianist for the workshop of a new opera\, Swimming in the Dark\, c
 omposed by Martin Hennessy with librettist Stephen Kitsakos. In fall 2024
 \, he performed a full guest recital at Jacobs with renowned European dou
 ble bass soloist Dominik Wagner. Liu was awarded a collaborative piano fe
 llowship at the Bowdoin International Music Festival in Brunswick\, Maine
 \, in the summer of 2025. This summer\, he joins the IU Summer String Aca
 demy as a faculty collaborative pianist. Liu began to study piano profess
 ionally with Ling Gao at age nine. At age 12\, he was admitted to the Aff
 iliated Middle School of Sichuan Conservatory of Music (China) with the f
 irst-place score. He studied with Chenggang Yang\, graduated as the first
 -place student\, and acquired his high school diploma there. He earned hi
 s Bachelor of Music degree from the School of Music at The University of 
 Kansas in 2019. He continued his Master of Music study there as a teachin
 g assistant and studied with Scott McBride Smith and Eric Zuber. In May 2
 024\, he completed a Graduate Artist Certificate as a teaching fellow at 
 the University of North Texas\, studying with Vladimir Viardo.
